<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Earth’s Moon · Grade 5 Space Explorer</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="../reader-navigation.css"></head><body><aside class="space-hub-bar" aria-label="Space Explorer topics"><a class="space-hub-home" href="../">← All space topics</a><div class="space-hub-links"><a href="../mars/">Mars</a><a href="../moon/" aria-current="page">Earth’s Moon</a><a href="../iss/">International Space Station</a><a href="../sun/">The Sun</a><a href="../constellations/">Constellations</a></div></aside><a class="skip-link" href="#reader">Skip to the reader</a><header><strong>SPACE EXPLORER / GRADE 5</strong><nav aria-label="Reader sections"><a href="#p1">01 Discover</a><a href="#p2">02 Explore</a><a href="#p3">03 Investigate</a><a href="#p4">04 Explore & Think</a></nav></header><noscript><p class="no-script">You can read the English article and open <a href="moon-reader.pdf">the illustrated PDF</a>. Enable JavaScript for translations and read-aloud.</p></noscript><div class="tools" role="region" aria-label="Language and reading controls"><label for="lang">Home language:</label><select id="lang"><option value="en">English</option><option value="zh-CN">中文</option><option value="ko-KR">한국어</option><option value="ja-JP">日本語</option><option value="de-DE">Deutsch</option></select><label for="section">Read section:</label><select id="section"><option value="0">1 · Discover</option><option value="1">2 · Explore</option><option value="2">3 · Investigate</option><option value="3">4 · Explore & Think</option></select><label for="voice">Reading voice:</label><select id="voice"><option value="">Device default</option></select><button id="read" type="button">▶ Read section</button><button id="pause" type="button" disabled>Ⅱ Pause</button><button id="stop" type="button" disabled>■ Stop</button><a class="pdf-link" href="moon-reader.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">↓ Open PDF</a><span class="current-section" id="current-section">Section 1: Discover</span><span id="status" aria-live="polite">Choose a section to hear it.</span></div><aside class="overview" id="overview" hidden aria-label="Home-language summary"><strong>Quick summary</strong><p id="summary-text"></p><small>Choose Translate under an English paragraph for home-language help. Read section plays this summary when a home language is selected.</small></aside><main id="reader"><section class="page" id="p1"><div class="hero"><img src="assets/images/moon-nearside.jpg" alt="NASA Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter camera mosaic of the Moon’s near side, showing dark lava plains and bright cratered highlands" width="1600" height="1555"><div class="hero-title"><div class="eyebrow" style="color:#ffd5a6">01 · Discover · SCIENCE READER 02</div><h1>EARTH’S<br>MOON</h1><p>Our neighbor in space · A rocky world with surprising secrets.</p></div></div><p class="cover-credit">Near-side mosaic from Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter camera data. NASA/Goddard Space Flight Center/Arizona State University. <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/resource/lunar-near-side/">NASA source</a></p><div class="content"><div class="eyebrow">01 / DISCOVER</div><h2>The neighbor that changes every night</h2><p class="readpara" data-p="0-0" style="font-size:20px">Look up tonight. You might see a bright circle, half a circle, or a thin silver curve. It is always the same Moon. So why does it seem to change?</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="0-0" aria-expanded="false">🌐 Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-0-0" hidden></p><p class="readpara" data-p="0-1">The Moon is Earth’s only natural satellite. That means it travels around our planet. It is about one-quarter as wide as Earth and roughly 384,400 kilometers away, on average.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="0-1" aria-expanded="false">🌐 Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-0-1" hidden></p><p class="readpara" data-p="0-2">The Moon does not make its own light. The sunlight you see is reflected from its surface. The Moon has followed Earth through space for billions of years. What stories could its rocks tell us?</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="0-2" aria-expanded="false">🌐 Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-0-2" hidden></p><div class="facts"><div class="fact"><b>384,400 km</b><small>Average distance from Earth</small></div><div class="fact"><b>27.3 days</b><small>One orbit around Earth</small></div><div class="fact"><b>29.5 days</b><small>New Moon to new Moon</small></div></div></div><div class="footer"><span>Earth’s Moon · Grade 5 Science · NASA science references</span><b>01 / 04</b></div></section><section class="page" id="p2"><div class="content"><div class="eyebrow">02 · Explore</div><h2>A WORLD OF CRATERS</h2><h3>What made all those holes?</h3><figure class="surface-photo"><img src="assets/images/moon-tycho.jpg" width="1800" height="827" alt="Rugged mountains in the center of Tycho crater cast long dark shadows across its floor" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Mountains rise from the center of Tycho crater in this LRO mosaic from June 10, 2011. Credit: NASA Goddard/Arizona State University. <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/resource/tycho-craters-central-peak-on-the-moon/">NASA source</a></figcaption></figure><p class="readpara" data-p="1-0">Imagine standing on the Moon. Under your boots is a layer of fine, gray dust. Around you are rocky hills and round pits called craters. Above you is a black sky—even during the lunar day.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="1-0" aria-expanded="false">🌐 Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-1-0" hidden></p><p class="readpara" data-p="1-1">Many craters formed when space rocks crashed into the Moon. Earth also gets hit, but wind, water, and moving land can erase old marks. The Moon has almost no atmosphere, rain, or wind, so many craters stay visible for a very long time.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="1-1" aria-expanded="false">🌐 Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-1-1" hidden></p><p class="readpara" data-p="1-2">You may notice dark patches on the Moon. They are not oceans. They are broad plains of cooled lava called maria. There are no rivers or oceans of liquid water on the Moon. But scientists have found evidence of water ice in some very cold, shadowed places near its poles.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="1-2" aria-expanded="false">🌐 Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-1-2" hidden></p><div class="callout"><b>LOOK CLOSELY</b> · The Moon’s dark areas are solid rock, not seas of water.</div></div><div class="footer"><span>Earth’s Moon · Grade 5 Science · NASA science references</span><b>02 / 04</b></div></section><section class="page" id="p3"><div class="content"><div class="eyebrow">03 · Investigate</div><h2>THE MOON CHANGES SHAPE—OR DOES IT?</h2><h3>Follow the sunlight</h3><img class="art" src="assets/moon-phases.svg" alt="Scientific diagram with new moon dark, first quarter right half lit, full moon fully lit, third quarter left half lit"><div class="caption">Four phases as seen from the Northern Hemisphere. Schematic diagram, not to scale; not a photograph. The Moon stays spherical. <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/moon/moon-phases/">NASA science</a></div><details class="phase-extra"><summary>Explore NASA’s diagram of all eight phases</summary><img src="assets/images/moon-phases.jpg" width="1800" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt="NASA phase diagram: an outer ring shows eight views from Earth, from new to full and back; a smaller ring shows the Moon orbiting Earth with the sunlit half facing the Sun"><p class="caption">Official explanatory diagram, not to scale. View from above Earth’s North Pole; outer images show the phases seen from Earth. Credit: NASA/Bill Dunford. <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/resource/phases-of-the-moon-2/">NASA source</a> · <a href="assets/images/moon-phases.jpg">Open a larger image</a></p></details><p class="readpara" data-p="2-0">One night the Moon is round. A week later it may look like half a circle. Did somebody take a bite out of it? Of course not! The Moon stays round all the time.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="2-0" aria-expanded="false">🌐 Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-2-0" hidden></p><p class="readpara" data-p="2-1">The Sun always lights about half the Moon. As the Moon orbits Earth, we see different amounts of the sunlit half. These changing views are called phases. A new moon is hard to see. A full moon shows us the whole sunlit face.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="2-1" aria-expanded="false">🌐 Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-2-1" hidden></p><p class="readpara" data-p="2-2">It takes about 29½ days to go from one new moon to the next. The regular phases are not caused by Earth’s shadow. A lunar eclipse is different: during a lunar eclipse, Earth’s shadow really does fall on the Moon.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="2-2" aria-expanded="false">🌐 Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-2-2" hidden></p><h3>Why the same side?</h3><p class="readpara" data-p="2-3">One mystery has a surprising answer: we see nearly the same side of the Moon from Earth because it spins once each time it travels once around Earth. The far side is not always dark—it gets sunlight too.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="2-3" aria-expanded="false">🌐 Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-2-3" hidden></p><div class="callout"><b>TRY IT</b> · Shine a lamp on a ball and walk around it. Watch how the bright part changes from where you stand.</div></div><div class="footer"><span>Earth’s Moon · Grade 5 Science · NASA science references</span><b>03 / 04</b></div></section><section class="page" id="p4"><div class="content"><div class="eyebrow">04 · Explore & Think</div><h2>FOOTPRINTS ON ANOTHER WORLD</h2><h3>How do we know?</h3><figure class="apollo-photo"><img src="assets/images/moon-apollo.jpg" width="1461" height="1600" alt="Buzz Aldrin in a white spacesuit on the Moon, with footprints in the dust and Neil Armstrong reflected in his visor" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Buzz Aldrin on the Moon during Apollo 11 in 1969, photographed by Neil Armstrong. Credit: NASA/MSFC. <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/resource/saturn-apollo-program-2/">NASA source</a></figcaption></figure><p class="readpara" data-p="3-0">In 1969, Apollo 11 astronauts Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin became the first people to walk on the Moon. They collected rocks, took photographs, and left footprints in the dusty ground.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="3-0" aria-expanded="false">🌐 Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-3-0" hidden></p><p class="readpara" data-p="3-1">Robotic orbiters study the Moon from above. Landers carry instruments to its surface. Scientists use their data, photographs, and rock samples to understand how the Moon formed and changed.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="3-1" aria-expanded="false">🌐 Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-3-1" hidden></p><h3>5 mission words</h3><div class="word-grid"><div class="word"><b>orbit</b>The path around another object</div><div class="word"><b>satellite</b>An object that goes around a planet</div><div class="word"><b>crater</b>A round hole left by an impact</div><div class="word"><b>phase</b>A view of the Moon’s sunlit part</div><div class="word"><b>reflect</b>To send light back</div></div><h3>Think like a scientist</h3><div class="question">1. Why can we see the Moon at night?</div><div class="question">2. Why do old craters stay visible for so long?</div><div class="question">3. What causes the phases of the Moon?</div><div class="question">4. Why is the Moon’s far side not always dark?</div><p style="font-size:12pt;margin-top:8px"><b>Sentence frames:</b> The Moon shines because ____. We see phases because ____.</p><div class="sources"><b>Explore the evidence</b><br><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/moon/facts/">NASA Moon Facts</a> · <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/moon/moon-phases/">NASA Moon Phases</a> · <a href="SOURCES-AND-IMAGE-CREDITS.md">Image and source credits</a></div></div><div class="footer"><span>Earth’s Moon · Grade 5 Science · NASA science references</span><b>04 / 04</b></div></section></main><script src="app.js" defer></script></body></html>